+### ISSUE 1 — CONSTRAINT_VIOLATION (PASS 1): the provisioning docs cover npm alone
+
+**Expert audit MUST-1** (`docs/superpowers/expert/2026-09-11-v3-6-wide-dispatch.md:520-524`): *"The
+author-facing rule MUST cover all five common ecosystems with exact commands, not npm alone … Each
+row MUST name the alternative that rewrites the lockfile."* **MUST-7** (`:544-547`) adds pnpm's
+trust-boundary condition.
+
+What actually shipped, across every author-facing surface:
+
+```
+$ git grep -n -i "poetry install|bundle install|go mod download|pnpm install|npm ci" \
+    -- (excluding audits, plans, specs and run dirs)
+TROUBLESHOOTING.md:113            provision_command: "npm ci"
+examples/manifest.example.yaml:34 provision_command: "npm ci"
+scripts/compound-v-run-*-worker.sh  # code comments: "npm ci, uv sync, bundle install"
+```
+
+`npm ci` is the only ecosystem an author is shown. There is no Python row, no Go row, no pnpm row
+anywhere in the repository, and **no** mention of `BUNDLE_FROZEN=true` — which the audit's own table
+(`:112`) singles out as the *only* safe Ruby form, because plain `bundle install` rewrites
+`Gemfile.lock` and the near-miss `bundle config set --local frozen true` writes `.bundle/config`,
+a sometimes-tracked file that trips MUST-2's own rule. The three worker scripts' comments name
+`bundle install` unqualified, which is the form the audit says not to use.
+
+**Failure scenario.** A Ruby project adopts `provision_command`. The author follows the only guidance
+that exists and writes `provision_command: "bundle install"`. It rewrites the tracked
+`Gemfile.lock`; the before-image lists untracked and ignored paths only, so the rewrite is never in
+the snapshot; the job is BLOCKED for a file it did not mean to touch, which is precisely the
+confusion MUST-6 exists to prevent — and it is BLOCKED on a tracked lockfile, so no
+`provision_error` is set to explain it either.
+
+**To fix.** Put the five-ecosystem table into the author-facing provisioning section of
+`skills/compound-v/execution-manifest.md`, with exact commands, the lockfile-rewriting alternative
+named per row, and pnpm's trust-boundary condition. The content already exists in the expert audit's
+table and only needs to be carried across.
+
+### ISSUE 2 — OVER_BUILD (PASS 1): per-job provisioning is explicitly out of scope and shipped anyway
+
+The spec's **Out of scope** section
+(`docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-11-v3.6-wide-dispatch-design.md`) names **"per-job provisioning"**.
+`_provision_spec` implements it:
+
+```python
+# scripts/compound-v-emit-workflow.py:1762-1772
+for source in (job or {}, manifest or {}):          # job FIRST
+    raw = source.get("provision_command") ...
+```
+
+It is live, not dead code. A manifest with **no** top-level `provision_command` and the key on the
+job entry provisions the worktree:
+
+```
+$ register-lane --isolation worktree ...   # manifest has only jobs[0].provision_command
+{ "provision": { "command": "mkdir -p node_modules/job-level && touch node_modules/job-level/MARKER",
+                 "rc": 0, "seconds": 0.009 } }
+$ ls <worktree>/node_modules/job-level/MARKER
+-rw-r--r--  0  MARKER
+```
+
+**And it is unvalidated.** `_validate_provision` reads `manifest.get(...)` only, and the validator
+rejects no unknown job key (`scripts/compound-v-validate-manifest.py:2659`). The same malformed
+values raise two violations at the top level and **zero** on the job:
+
+```
+$ validate-manifest <top-level provision_command: 3, provision_timeout_s: 0>
+  - manifest 'provision_command' must be a non-empty, single-line string (got 3)
+  - manifest 'provision_timeout_s' must be an integer between 1 and 1800 (got 0)
+
+$ validate-manifest <the SAME values on the job entry>
+  - manifest missing required top-level field 'spec_path'      # four unrelated violations,
+  - ... 'plan_path' / 'audits' / 'max_parallel'                 # and nothing about provisioning
+```
+
+**Failure scenario.** A multi-line `provision_command` on a job entry passes the validator clean —
+the single-line check that exists at top level never runs — and `register-lane` hands the whole
+string to `/bin/bash -c` inside the worktree. The top-level key is validated precisely because a
+multi-line value is dangerous (`compound-v-validate-manifest.py:5951` has the selftest row:
+`"npm ci\nrm -rf /"`). The job-level path has no such row and no such check.
+
+`skills/compound-v/execution-manifest.md:184-186` documents the behaviour honestly — *"A job entry
+may carry its own pair, which the emitter prefers over the manifest-level one; the validator checks
+the top-level pair."* That is good documentation of something the spec said not to build, and the
+asymmetry it discloses is the defect.
+
+**To fix — the implementer's call, not the reviewer's, but two shapes exist.** Either drop the
+job-level read from `_provision_spec` so the code matches the spec's Out of scope and remove the
+sentence from `execution-manifest.md`; or, if per-job provisioning is genuinely wanted, take it out
+of Out of scope deliberately and give the job-level pair the same validation and the same
+planted-failure selftest rows the top-level pair has.
+
+### Not blocking
+
+The four QUALITY observations stand and none of them gates DONE. The one worth its own task is
+observation 1: `usage.advisor_calls` is correct, well-tested code with no end-to-end path, because no
+emitted workflow calls `write_workflow_usage`. That predates 3.6.0 and should not be bolted onto the
+fix round for the two issues above.
